WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:04.960
More than 35 years after The Empire Strikes
Back was released, there's something about

00:00:04.960 --> 00:00:08.020
the Star Wars sequel that still doesn't sit
right with Mark Hamill.

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:13.200
At the beginning of the 1980 blockbuster,
Mark Hamill's iconic character Luke Skywalker

00:00:13.200 --> 00:00:17.240
is captured by a massive snow beast called
a Wampa on the icy planet of Hoth.

00:00:17.380 --> 00:00:21.660
To get away, Skywalker uses his lightsaber
to slice off one of the creature's arms.

00:00:21.660 --> 00:00:24.200
But that apparently wasn't what Hamill signed
up for.

00:00:24.320 --> 00:00:28.300
Hamill revealed on Twitter he was originally
under the impression that Luke wouldn't actually

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:29.310
hurt the Wampa.

00:00:29.310 --> 00:00:33.440
"When filming [the] scene, I was assured my
lightsaber swipe toward camera ([the] creature

00:00:33.440 --> 00:00:36.500
[was] not on set) would simply singe [its]
fur [to] scare him off.

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:40.120
Horrified to later see amputation and unnecessary
cruelty.

00:00:40.300 --> 00:00:42.000
Wampa was hungry (not evil).

00:00:42.180 --> 00:00:43.320
Luke would never do this!"

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:47.420
The comments echo statements Hamill has made
about the scene in the past on his show Mark

00:00:47.420 --> 00:00:48.780
Hamill's Pop Culture Quest.

00:00:49.260 --> 00:00:51.980
"Then I remember saying, 'Well, I'm just grazing his
fur, right?'

00:00:52.980 --> 00:00:53.620
They said, 'what, what do you mean?

00:00:53.620 --> 00:00:56.240
I said, well, it's like a hungry bear, he's
not evil.

00:00:56.320 --> 00:00:56.820
"Yeah."

00:00:57.180 --> 00:00:58.500
"So I should just scare him off."

00:00:58.680 --> 00:01:06.910
"Not only did I cut off its arm they did a
insert of it falling in slomotion, I just - I never thought

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:08.300
that was right."

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:13.140
The topic came up when a fan shared a photo
of a cake made to look like a severed Wampa

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:14.510
arm for National Cake Day.

00:01:14.600 --> 00:01:19.020
But Hamill, an outspoken animal lover, said
he's still angry about the scene.

00:01:19.020 --> 00:01:23.560
Of course, the special edition of Empire Strikes
Back has an even more graphic version with

00:01:23.560 --> 00:01:25.420
the Wampa shown bloody and screaming.

00:01:25.740 --> 00:01:28.400
A Twitter user responded that it all seemed
gratuitous.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:29.900
"Heck, it was even in the book.

00:01:30.200 --> 00:01:32.520
Luke used the force to sense the Wampa's mind."

00:01:32.520 --> 00:01:35.200
Hamill replied, "My acting was much better
in the book."

00:01:35.520 --> 00:01:39.610
This isn't the first time Luke's battle with
the Wampa has made headlines, as a deleted

00:01:39.610 --> 00:01:44.000
scene from Empire Strikes Back seemed to suggest
the fight was added to explain the facial

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.360
scars Hamill suffered in a 1977 car accident.

00:01:49.780 --> 00:01:50.960
"Bacta is growing real well.

00:01:51.500 --> 00:01:53.900
. Scars oughta be gone in a day or so."

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:58.120
We'll find out how Luke feels about a whole
new batch of creatures when Star Wars: The

00:01:58.120 --> 00:02:00.840
Last Jedi hits theaters on December 15.

00:02:00.980 --> 00:02:03.500
Hopefully, he's kinder to Porgs than Chewbacca
is.

00:02:04.260 --> 00:02:04.960
Thanks for watching!

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:08.100
Click the Looper icon to subscribe to our YouTube channel.

00:02:08.140 --> 00:02:10.500
Plus check out all this cool stuff we know you'll love, too!

